'''transition''' (trȧn-sish′ọn), ''n.'' [< F. ''transition'' = Pr. ''transitio'' = Sp. ''transicion'' = Pg. ''transição'' = It. ''transizione'', < L. ''transitio(n-)'', a passing over or away, < ''transire'', go or pass over: see ''transient'', ''transit.''] 1. Passage from one place, state, or act to another; change: as, a sudden ''transition'' from anger to mirth; a state of ''transition.''

{{smaller block|Thence, by a soft ''transition'', we repair<br>From earthly vehicles to these of air.
{{right|''Pope'', R. of the L., i. 49.}}}}

{{smaller block|What sprightly ''transitions'' does she make from an opera or a sermon to an ivory comb or a pincushion!
{{right|''Addison'', Spectator, No. 45.}}}}

{{smaller block|When Bunyan passed from this horrible condition [of doubt] into a state of happy feeling, his mind was nearly overthrown by the ''transition.'' ''Southey'', Bunyan, p. 33.}}

2. In ''rhet.'', a passing from one subject to another.

{{smaller block|So here the archangel paused<br>Betwixt the world destroy'd and world restored. . . .<br>Then, with ''transition'' sweet, new speech resumes.
{{right|''Milton'', P. L., xii. 5.}}}}

3. In ''music'', same (usually) as ''modulation.'' {{smaller|Sometimes, however, the term is used more precisely either for a sudden, abrupt shift from one tonality to another unrelated to it, or for a modulation without}}

readings.c3s2:
{{smaller|change of mode. The latter is the technical usage of the tonic sol-faists.}}

'''4.''' In ''geol.'', the English form of the name (used attributively or as an adjective) given by Werner to certain strata which he investigated in northern Germany, and found to have, to a certain extent, the mineral character of the so-called primitive rocks, while also exhibiting indications of a mechanical origin, and even containing occasional fossils, thus indicating a transition or passage from primary to secondary. {{smaller|The name was afterward extended so as to embrace rocks of similar character in other regions. The argillaceous sandstone called by the Germans ''grauwacke'' (see ''graywacke'') formed a part of the transition formation, and it was the rocks previously called ''grauwacke'' and ''transition limestone'' which Murchison studied in England and Wales, and to which, having worked out their order of succession, he gave the name of ''Silurian.'' See ''Silurian.''}}

'''5.''' In ''art hist.'', an epoch or stage of change from one style or state of development in art to the next succeeding; especially, in Greek art, the stage of change from the archaic to the bloom of art, and in medieval art, that from

